Both research threads point the same direction. The local-repo check found no existing JTS/GEOS/GeoTools metadata (JTS currently only appears transitively, as a dependency type used by h2gis), and confirmed the baseline scaffold is just `testImplementation` on the library plus AssertJ — nothing special. The external-research agent couldn't reach the network in this sandboxed session (web tools are denied for everyone here, not just me), but it's working from well-established knowledge of JTS that matches my own: JTS (`org.locationtech.jts:jts-core`) is a from-scratch pure-Java reimplementation of the OGC Simple Features geometry model — notably, it's GEOS (the C++ library) that was ported *from* JTS, not the other way around, so there's no native/JNI dependency to worry about. `jts-core` declares no non-test runtime dependencies, and its API (`GeometryFactory`, `PrecisionModel`, WKT/WKB readers/writers) is plain constructor/method calls with no environment or system-property configuration, no database, and no Docker service involved.

`★ Insight ─────────────────────────────────────`
A good tell for "does this library need Forge setup" is asking whether the library's own value proposition *is* wrapping something external (a native engine, a DB driver, a message broker). JTS's whole pitch is the opposite — it exists so JVM code doesn't need a native geometry engine. That's a strong structural signal pointing toward `no_action`, independent of any single dependency check.
`─────────────────────────────────────────────────`

This is a case for `no_action` — the generated scaffold (JUnit + the `jts-core` test dependency) is sufficient to reach meaningful coverage; nothing here needs deterministic Maven/Docker setup or special env/system-property guidance.

